 "incomparability_note": "W1 (the call contract) and W2 (the wire encoding) are Blackwell-incomparable because they\nreveal logically independent coordinates of the latent module spec S. (1) W2 is not a\ngarbling of W1: no Markov kernel applied to the call-contract signal alone can output the\ncents-scale, the '#' separator, or the (sum-of-digits mod 10) check digit -- those are facts\nabsent from W1's sigma-algebra, so producing them would inject information the kernel does\nnot possess. (2) W1 is not a garbling of W2: no kernel on the wire-encoding signal alone can\noutput the private argument order (amount-first), the required keyword-only memo, or the\nLedgerRejection exception type. Neither signal refines the other, so neither sufficiency\ndirection holds -> incomparable. The dominance control is the contrast: W1plus = W2 ++ W1\nliterally contains W2's signal, so the projection 'drop the contract sentences' IS a valid\ngarbling W1plus -> W2, giving W1plus >=_B W2 one-directionally. CONFOUND DEFENSE: the two\nsources are matched on every nuisance axis (length, one-unguessable-fact-each, same module,\nsame short solution.py + snippet-verifier task shape); the ONLY thing varied is which\ncoordinate of S is revealed. Task difficulty is ruled out by the DOUBLE crossover at MATCHED\nNEAR-ZERO no-context floors (pre-registered and measured first): if the encode family were\nmerely 'harder', W1 would not lift it AND W2 would still not lift the api family -- but each\nsource lifts ONLY its own family from the same ~0 floor, so the lift is information-specific,\nnot difficulty-specific.",
